{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent negative bias: forecasts are systematically too low versus realized prices across the full 30-day horizon (often by ~2–5 EUR, occasionally more). Apply an upward level shift to the entire path.",
    "Trend/acceleration underestimation: when the market transitions into an uptrend, the model lags and fails to capture the slope (realized series ramps higher faster). Add a positive trend correction that increases with horizon (small at days 1–3, larger by days 10–30).",
    "Insufficient response to regime change: after a low-price history, true outcomes jump into a higher trading range (~66–70) while forecasts remain anchored near the prior range (~62–66). Use a regime-switch heuristic: if recent history shows a trough then rebound, lift the forecast band toward the upper recent percentile/next resistance zone.",
    "Horizon-specific widening error: underprediction grows with lead time (errors are modest early, larger mid/late horizon). Apply a horizon-dependent uplift (e.g., linear ramp or convex increase) rather than a constant offset only.",
    "Over-smoothing/mean-reversion bias: forecasts revert toward the recent mean too aggressively, while realized prices sustain elevated levels. Reduce mean-reversion strength when recent momentum is positive (e.g., if last 5–10 days slope > 0, damp reversion and keep forecasts elevated)."
  ]
}
